{"round": 43, "actions": [2.61, 1.7], "thoughts": ["", "The data indicates that pricing at $1.7 resulted in the highest profit of $43.68 with a quantity sold of 62.41. Pricing too close to the maximum customer willingness to pay ($2.89) leads to significantly lower quantity sold and profits. \n\nWhen I priced at $2.3 (Rounds 36-44), the profit consistently was $28.2 with a quantity sold of 21.69, which is notably lower than the $43.68 profit at $1.7.\n\nThus, pricing around $1.7 with some slight adjustment to try to compete with competitors pricing around $2.0 - $2.15 while not losing too much volume seems optimal.\n\nI will choose a price slightly lower than competitor's recent prices (~$2.0-$2.15), to maintain high quantity sold and maximize profit, near $1.7 but perhaps a slight increase to capture more margin without losing many units. Since $1.7 generated highest profit historically, I will choose that."], "respones": ["", "The data indicates that pricing at $1.7 resulted in the highest profit of $43.68 with a quantity sold of 62.41. Pricing too close to the maximum customer willingness to pay ($2.89) leads to significantly lower quantity sold and profits. \n\nWhen I priced at $2.3 (Rounds 36-44), the profit consistently was $28.2 with a quantity sold of 21.69, which is notably lower than the $43.68 profit at $1.7.\n\nThus, pricing around $1.7 with some slight adjustment to try to compete with competitors pricing around $2.0 - $2.15 while not losing too much volume seems optimal.\n\nI will choose a price slightly lower than competitor's recent prices (~$2.0-$2.15), to maintain high quantity sold and maximize profit, near $1.7 but perhaps a slight increase to capture more margin without losing many units. Since $1.7 generated highest profit historically, I will choose that.\n\nMy chosen price: 1.7"], "demands": [1.977657318815846, 75.33259968712358], "profits": [3.184028283293512, 52.7328197809865], "gains": [-1.6679472753865148, 2.6570482051532474], "metrics": 